WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.340
Choosing a model for Apple Silicon starts with the workload.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.520
A casual assistant, coding helper, summarizer, document reviewer,

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.450
and extraction workflow may each need different model behavior.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.680
Model size and parameter count matter, but they are not the whole story.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.580
A smaller instruction-tuned model with the right prompt format can be more useful than a

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.750
larger model that is slow or poorly matched.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.820
Quantization level is a practical memory and quality decision.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.650
More aggressive quantization can reduce RAM requirements and make a model fit on a

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.530
lower-memory Mac,

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.090
but it may reduce reasoning quality, instruction following, or consistency.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.190
Less aggressive quantization may improve quality but require more memory and run more

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:34.695
slowly.

00:00:34.695 --> 00:00:38.030
Testing realistic prompts is better than choosing by filename alone.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.000
Context length should be selected for the task.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.570
Long-document workflows may need more context,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.360
but larger context windows increase memory use and prompt processing time.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.640
If the workflow is short chat, a very large context setting may simply waste resources.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.740
Apple Silicon can be strong for local workflows, but model size, context length,

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.280
runtime overhead, and other applications still compete for unified memory.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.980
Licensing and provenance matter.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.860
Read model cards where available, track the source of model files,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.620
and understand whether the license permits your intended use.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.750
Local execution does not erase license obligations or supply chain concerns.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.350
A good model choice aligns memory, speed, quality, licensing, trust,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.080
and privacy needs with the hardware actually available.
